s, that's the typesystem implementation. So string = content[0:4] = \"a: 1\".\n\n`.value` for DictToken: `_get_value` returns `{key._value: value._value for ...}`? Actually in typesystem:\n\n```python\nclass DictToken(Token):\n    def _get_value(self) -> typing.Any:\n        return {\n            key._get_value(): value._get_value()\n            for key, value in self._value.items()\n        }\n```\n\nSince construct_mapping returns a dict whose keys/values are already Token instances (because the constructors for scalars return ScalarToken), the DictToken._value is a dict of Token->Token. So `.value` gives {\"a\": 1}.\n\nFor nested: `{\"a\": {\"b\": [1, 2]}}` etc.\n\nLet me design tests:\n\n```python\nimport pytest\nimport yaml\nfrom typesystem.base import ParseError, Position\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml import tokenize_yaml\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokens import DictToken, ListToken, ScalarToken\n```\n\nTest 1: bytes input with mapping:\n```python\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_from_bytes():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(b'{\"a\": 1}')\n    assert isinstance(token, DictToken)\n    assert token.value == {\"a\": 1}\n    assert token.string == '{\"a\": 1}'\n    assert token.start == Position(line_no=1, column_no=1, char_index=0)\n    assert token.end == Position(line_no=1, column_no=8, char_index=7)\n```\n\nWait, end index: for flow mapping `{\"a\": 1}`, start_mark.index=0, end_mark.index=8 (after `}`). So end_index = 7. Position for index 7: line 1, column 8. Position equality: Position has `__eq__`? Let me recall:\n\n```python\nclass Position:\n    def __init__(self, line_no: int, column_no: int, char_index: int):\n        ...\n    def __eq__(self, other: typing.Any) -> bool:\n        return (\n            isinstance(other, Position)\n            and self.line_no == other.line_no\n            and self.column_no == other.column_no\n            and self.char_index == other.char_index\n        )\n```\n\nI believe Position has __eq__ in typesystem. Yes, it does. But to be safe, I could compare attributes individually. I'll compare attributes to be safe: `token.end.char_index == 7` etc. Actually comparing Position objects with == should be fine, but attribute comparison is safest. I'll do attribute comparisons.\n\nTest 2: empty string:\n```python\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_empty_string():\n    with pytest.raises(ParseError) as exc:\n        tokenize_yaml(\"\")\n    assert exc.value.text == \"No content.\"\n    assert exc.value.code == \"no_content\"\n    assert exc.value.position == Position(line_no=1, column_no=1, char_index=0)\n```\n\nAlso whitespace-only: `tokenize_yaml(\"   \\n  \")` \u2014 same. And bytes empty: `tokenize_yaml(b\"\")` covers bytes branch + empty branch. Actually bytes branch is covered by test 1. But empty bytes covers both decode and empty. Fine.\n\nTest 3: mapping (construct_mapping):\n```python\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_mapping():\n    content = \"a: 1\\nb: two\"\n    token = tokenize_yaml(content)\n    assert isinstance(token, DictToken)\n    assert token.value == {\"a\": 1, \"b\": \"two\"}\n    assert token.string == content\n    # start index 0, end index len(content)-1\n    assert token.start.char_index == 0\n    assert token.end.char_index == len(content) - 1\n```\n\nWait: for block mapping \"a: 1\\nb: two\", the mapping node end_mark.index = 11 (len of content). end_index = 10 = len-1. Good.\n\nAlso check nested token lookup: `token.lookup([\"a\"])` returns ScalarToken with value 1. Hmm, lookup uses `_get_child_token`. For DictToken:\n\n```python\ndef _get_child_token(self, key):\n    if self._child_keys is None:\n        ...\n    token = self._child_tokens.get(key, ...)\n```\n\nActually let me not rely too much on lookup internals; I can check `token._get_child_token(\"a\")` hmm, that's private. The public API: `lookup(index: list)`. For DictToken, lookup with [\"a\"] returns the child token. Let me keep it simple and just check value/string/start/end, plus isinstance of nested tokens via `token.lookup([\"a\"])`.\n\nActually, to verify construct_int etc. were used, the child values should be ScalarToken instances. `token.lookup([\"a\"])` \u2014 for DictToken, `lookup` implementation:\n\n```python\ndef lookup(self, index: list) -> Token:\n    if self._child_keys is None:\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, I don't remember exactly. Let me check Token.lookup from typesystem source:\n\n```python\ndef lookup(self, index: list) -> Token:\n    token = self\n    for key in index:\n        if isinstance(token, DictToken):\n            token = token._get_child_token(key)\n        elif isinstance(token, ListToken):\n            token = token._get_child_token(key)\n        else:\n            raise AssertionError(...)\n    return token\n```\n\nSomething like that. And `_get_child_token` for DictToken:\n\n```python\ndef _get_child_token(self, key: typing.Any) -> Token:\n    return self._child_tokens[key]\n```\n\nHmm, but `_child_tokens` maps `k._value: v` where k is the key token. So `token.lookup([\"a\"])` returns the ScalarToken for value 1. I think that works. But to be safe, maybe I should avoid lookup and instead access `token._value` keys which are Tokens. Since construct_mapping returns dict with Token keys and Token values (because scalar constructors return ScalarToken). So:\n\n```python\nkey_token = next(iter(token._value))  # ScalarToken \"a\"\nvalue_token = token._value[key_token]  # ScalarToken 1\nassert isinstance(value_token, ScalarToken)\nassert value_token.value == 1\n```\n\nHmm, using private `_value` is a bit ugly but reliable. Alternatively use `lookup`. I recall typesystem's Token.lookup:\n\n```python\ndef lookup(self, index: list) -> Token:\n    from typesystem.tokenize.tokens import DictToken, ListToken\n\n    token = self\n    for key in index:\n        if isinstance(token, DictToken):\n            token = token._get_child_token(key)\n        elif isinstance(token, ListToken):\n            token = token._get_child_token(key)\n        else:\n            raise AssertionError(f\"No token for {key} in {token}\")\n    return token\n```\n\nAnd DictToken._get_child_token:\n\n```python\ndef _get_child_token(self, key: typing.Any) -> Token:\n    if self._child_tokens is None:\n        return ...\n    return self._child_tokens[key]\n```\n\nHmm wait, actually in typesystem the DictToken init:\n\n```python\ndef __init__(self, *args, **kwargs):\n    super().__init__(*args, **kwargs)\n    self._child_keys = None\n    self._child_tokens = None\n```\n\nand `_get_value` builds the dict... Hmm, there are different versions. The get_info output shows:\n\n```python\nclass DictToken(Token):\n    def __init__(self, *args, **kwargs):\n        super().__init__(*args, **kwargs)\n        self._child_keys = {k._value: k for k in self._value.keys()}\n        self._child_tokens = {k._value: v for k, v in self._value.items()}\n```\n\nSo `_child_tokens` maps key value -> value token. And `_get_child_token(key)` returns `self._child_tokens[key]`. And `lookup([\"a\"])` should work. And `value` property:\n\n```python\n@property\ndef value(self):\n    return self._get_value()\n```\n\nDictToken._get_value:\n\n```python\ndef _get_value(self):\n    return {key: token._get_value() for key, token in self._child_tokens.items()}\n```\n\nSomething like that. OK.\n\nTo be safe, I'll primarily assert on `.value`, `.string`, `.start`, `.end`, and use `lookup` for nested checks but wrap in a way that if lookup fails the test would fail \u2014 that's fine, it's a test. But I want the tests to be correct. Let me be conservative: use `.value` and `.string` and start/end, and isinstance checks on the top token. For nested token type checks, I can use `token.lookup([\"a\"])` \u2014 I'm fairly confident this works in typesystem. Actually, let me double check `lookup` in typesystem tokenize/tokens.py:\n\n```python\nclass Token:\n    ...\n    def lookup(self, index: list) -> Token:\n        token = self\n        for key in index:\n            if isinstance(token, DictToken):\n                token = token._get_child_token(key)\n            elif isinstance(token, ListToken):\n                token = token._get_child_token(key)\n            else:\n                raise AssertionError(f\"Cannot lookup {key!r} in {token!r}.\")\n        return token\n```\n\nHmm, I'm not 100% sure of the exact implementation but lookup exists per get_info. The risk: `_get_child_token` for DictToken might return `self._child_tokens.get(key)` or raise KeyError. Either way, for existing keys it works.\n\nActually, I realize I should just call get_info on Token.lookup... but the user said stop calling tools. OK, I'll write conservative tests.\n\nConservative approach: assert on value, string, start/end positions computed from content. For nested types, I can check via `token._value` \u2014 but that's private too. Hmm. Actually `lookup` is public API of typesystem tokens, used in error highlighting. I'll use it but keep assertions on `.value` of the returned token.\n\nLet me now compute positions carefully for each test.\n\nTest: mapping block style:\ncontent = \"a: 1\\nb: two\" (len 11)\n- Mapping node: start_mark.index = 0, end_mark.index = 11. DictToken(\u2026, 0, 10, content). string = content[0:11] = content. \n- start Position: char_index 0, line 1, column 1.\n- end Position: char_index 10, line 2, column 4 (\"two\" ends at index 10, column: index - rfind('\\n',0,10) = 10 - 3 = 7? wait rfind('\\n', 0, 10): content = \"a: 1\\nb: two\", indices: a0 :1 space2 13 \\n4 b5 :6 space7 t8 w9 o10. rfind('\\n',0,10) = 4. column = 10-4 = 6. line_no = count('\\n',0,10)+1 = 2. So end Position(line_no=2, column_no=6, char_index=10).\n\nHmm wait, Position._get_position in Token:\n\n```python\ndef _get_position(self, index: int) -> Position:\n    return Position(\n        line_no=self._content.count(\"\\n\", 0, index) + 1,\n        column_no=index - self._content.rfind(\"\\n\", 0, index),\n        char_index=index,\n    )\n```\n\nAnd `start` property returns `self._get_position(self._start_index)`, `end` returns `self._get_position(self._end_index)`. Hmm, actually I recall:\n\n```python\n@property\ndef start(self) -> Position:\n    return self._get_position(self._start_index)\n\n@property\ndef end(self) -> Position:\n    return self._get_position(self._end_index + 1)\n```\n\nHmm, is it end_index + 1? Let me think. In typesystem source (tokenize/tokens.py):\n\n```python\n    @property\n    def start(self) -> Position:\n        return self._get_position(self._start_index)\n\n    @property\n    def end(self) -> Position:\n        return self._get_position(self._end_index + 1)\n```\n\nI believe end is `_get_position(self._end_index + 1)` because end_index is inclusive. So for DictToken(\u2026, 0, 10), end = position of index 11 = char_index 11, line 2, column 7 (11 - 4 = 7). Hmm, that's one past the last char.\n\nI'm not certain. To avoid brittleness, I'll assert on `char_index` only where I'm confident, or avoid asserting exact end positions and instead assert `token.string == content` and start.char_index == 0. Actually, let me just assert start and end char_index with the inclusive convention: start.char_index == 0, end.char_index == len(content) - 1. If end uses _end_index + 1, then end.char_index == len(content). Risky.\n\nSafest: assert `token.string == content` (string = content[start:end+1], robust to the end property convention) and `token.start.char_index == 0`. And maybe assert `token.end.char_index in (len(content)-1, len(content))`? That's ugly. \n\nAlternative: don't assert end position at all; assert string and value. The string property: `self._content[self._start_index : self._end_index + 1]` \u2014 I'm fairly confident about this one since it's the standard implementation. Let me double-check by recalling typesystem's tokens.py:\n\n```python\n    @property\n    def string(self) -> str:\n        if self._content is None:\n            return \"\"\n        return self._content[self._start_index : self._end_index + 1]\n```\n\nYes, I'm confident.\n\nOK here's my plan for assertions per test:\n- isinstance checks\n- token.value == expected\n- token.string == expected substring\n- token.start.char_index == expected start\n- token.end.char_index == expected end (inclusive) \u2014 I'll include this since _end_index is directly stored; `end` property might add 1 though. Hmm.\n\nLet me think again about the `end` property. From typesystem GitHub (encode/typesystem), tokenize/tokens.py:\n\n```python\nclass Token:\n    def __init__(self, value, start_index, end_index, content=\"\"):\n        self._value = value\n        self._start_index = start_index\n        self._end_index = end_index\n        self._content = content\n\n    @property\n    def start(self) -> Position:\n        return self._get_position(self._start_index)\n\n    @property\n    def end(self) -> Position:\n        return self._get_position(self._end_index + 1)\n```\n\nI'm fairly sure `end` uses `self._end_index + 1`. Yes \u2014 in typesystem, positions are exclusive-end for the `end` property. E.g., in their tests: for `{\"a\": 1}` content, token.end.char_index == 8 (len). Hmm.\n\nGiven uncertainty, I'll assert `token.end.char_index == len(content)` for full-content tokens? If end property is `_end_index` without +1, that would fail. Ugh.\n\nDecision: avoid asserting `.end` exactly; instead assert `.string` (which pins both indices) and `.start.char_index == 0`. The string property pins start_index and end_index precisely: string == content[start:end+1]. If string == full content, then start=0 and end+1=len, i.e., end_index = len-1. That's a strong assertion. \n\nFor nested tokens, e.g. lookup([\"a\"]).string == \"1\" for content \"a: 1\". The scalar node for `1` in \"a: 1\": start_mark.index = 3, end_mark.index = 4. ScalarToken(value, 3, 3). string = content[3:4] = \"1\". Good.\n\nFor \"b: two\": scalar \"two\" start=5? content \"a: 1\\nb: two\": indices t=8,w=9,o=10. start_mark.index=8, end_mark.index=11. string = content[8:12] = \"two\". Good.\n\nNow, is `lookup` safe? Let me reconsider. typesystem Token.lookup:\n\n```python\n    def lookup(self, index: list) -> Token:\n        token = self\n        for key in index:\n            if isinstance(token, DictToken):\n                token = token._get_child_token(key)\n            elif isinstance(token, ListToken):\n                token = token._get_child_token(key)\n            else:\n                raise AssertionError(f\"No token for {key} in {token}.\")\n        return token\n```\n\nAnd DictToken._get_child_token:\n\n```python\n    def _get_child_token(self, key: typing.Any) -> Token:\n        return self._child_tokens[key]\n```\n\nGiven the get_info output shows `_child_tokens = {k._value: v for k, v in self._value.items()}`, `_get_child_token(\"a\")` returns the value token for key \"a\". I'm reasonably confident. I'll use lookup for nested assertions but keep them simple (value and string).\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 there's a subtlety with DictToken._get_value. The get_info shows `_get_value` returns \"...\" (omitted). The actual implementation:\n\n```python\n    def _get_value(self) -> typing.Any:\n        return {\n            key: token._get_value()\n            for key, token in self._child_tokens.items()\n        }\n```\n\nHmm, or maybe:\n\n```python\n    def _get_value(self) -> typing.Any:\n        return {\n            key._get_value(): value._get_value()\n            for key, value in self._value.items()\n        }\n```\n\nEither way, `token.value == {\"a\": 1, \"b\": \"two\"}` should hold. Good.\n\nNow the parse error tests:\n\nScannerError: content = \"a: 'unclosed\" \u2014 yaml scanner raises ScannerError with problem \"found unexpected end of stream\" and problem_mark at index 12 (end). Actually for unclosed single quote: \"while scanning a quoted scalar ... found unexpected end of stream\". problem_mark.index = 12 (len of content). Then text = \"found unexpected end of stream.\" and position char_index = 12, line 1, column 13.\n\nLet me verify: content = \"a: 'unclosed\" (12 chars). The scanner scans the quoted scalar starting at index 3, reaches EOF at index 12, raises ScannerError with problem_mark at index 12. So position.char_index == 12, line_no = 1, column_no = 12 - rfind('\\n',0,12) = 12 - (-1) = 13. rfind returns -1 when not found. So column_no = 13.\n\nI'll assert code == \"parse_error\", text == \"found unexpected end of stream.\", position.char_index == 12. Hmm, text assertion depends on pyyaml version \u2014 \"found unexpected end of stream\" has been stable for ages. I'll assert it but maybe more loosely: `exc.value.text.endswith(\".\")` and `\"unexpected end of stream\" in exc.value.text`? To be safe but still meaningful: assert exc.value.code == \"parse_error\" and exc.value.text == \"found unexpected end of stream.\" \u2014 I'm confident this is the pyyaml problem string. Actually, let me double check pyyaml scanner for unclosed quote:\n\n```python\ndef scan_flow_scalar(self, token_class, single):\n    ...\n    while True:\n        ...\n        if not self.flow_level and self.check_plain()...\n```\n\nThe error: `raise ScannerError(\"while scanning a quoted scalar\", start_mark, \"found unexpected end of stream\", self.get_mark())`. Yes, problem = \"found unexpected end of stream\". Good.\n\nParserError: content = \"{a: 1\" \u2014 flow mapping unclosed. ParserError: \"while parsing a flow mapping ... expected ',' or '}', but got ...\" Hmm, actually for \"{a: 1\" at EOF: the parser expects ',' or '}' but gets StreamEndToken. problem = \"expected ',' or '}', but got '<stream end>'\"? Hmm, the problem text includes token repr. Let me instead use a simpler parser error: content = \"a: 1\\n b: 2\"? No...\n\nAlternative parser error: \"foo: bar: baz\" \u2192 ParserError \"mapping values are not allowed here\"? Actually that's a ScannerError? \"mapping values are not allowed here\" is a ScannerError in pyyaml (scanner.py, scan_value). Hmm.\n\nLet me think: `yaml.load(\"[1, 2