ruct_mapping(node, deep=False)? In pyyaml, `construct_mapping(self, node, deep=False)`:\n\n```python\ndef construct_mapping(self, node, deep=False):\n    ...\n    mapping = {}\n    for key_node, value_node in node.value:\n        key = self.construct_object(key_node, deep=deep)\n        if not isinstance(key, collections.abc.Hashable):\n            raise ...\n        value = self.construct_object(value_node, deep=deep)\n        mapping[key] = value\n    return mapping\n```\n\nKeys/values are constructed via construct_object \u2192 our constructors \u2192 Tokens. \u2713. Note: with deep=False, nested collections may be generators... Actually construct_object for a non-scalar node with deep=False returns the partially-constructed object; for mappings/sequences pyyaml fills them in later via construct_document. Hmm \u2014 wait, this is important. In pyyaml, construct_object(node, deep=False):\n\n```python\ndef construct_object(self, node, deep=False):\n    if node in self.constructed_objects:\n        return self.constructed_objects[node]\n    if deep:\n        old_deep = self.deep_construct\n        self.deep_construct = True\n    if node in self.recursive_objects:\n        raise ConstructorError(...)\n    self.recursive_objects[node] = None\n    constructor = None\n    ...\n    if constructor is None:\n        constructor = self.__class__.construct_mapping? no...\n    data = constructor(self, node)  # our construct_mapping \u2192 returns DictToken immediately\n    if isinstance(data, types.GeneratorType):\n        generator = data\n        data = next(generator)\n        if self.deep_construct:\n            for dummy in generator:\n                pass\n        else:\n            self.state_generators.append(generator)\n    self.constructed_objects[node] = data\n    ...\n```\n\nOur construct_mapping calls `loader.construct_mapping(node)` which returns a complete dict (construct_mapping itself constructs keys and values with construct_object(key_node, deep=False)... hmm, wait:\n\nBaseConstructor.construct_mapping:\n\n```python\n    def construct_mapping(self, node, deep=False):\n        if not isinstance(node, MappingNode):\n            raise ConstructorError(...)\n        mapping = {}\n        for key_node, value_node in node.value:\n            key = self.construct_object(key_node, deep=deep)\n            if not isinstance(key, collections.abc.Hashable):\n                raise ConstructorError(...)\n            value = self.construct_object(value_node, deep=deep)\n            mapping[key] = value\n        return mapping\n```\n\nWith deep=False, for a nested mapping value_node, construct_object calls our construct_mapping which calls loader.construct_mapping(node) \u2014 recursion happens eagerly here since our constructor calls construct_mapping synchronously (not a generator). So nested dicts are fully built. The generator concern applies only to pyyaml's own yield-based constructors (construct_yaml_map yields). Our constructors return directly, so everything is eager. \u2713. That's why the original code works.\n\nBut note: DictToken keys must be hashable Tokens \u2014 ScalarToken defines __hash__ (per get_info). \u2713.\n\nAlso note: for duplicate handling etc., fine.\n\nNow \u2014 `mapping[key] = value` where key is a ScalarToken. Then DictToken init: `{k._value: k for k in self._value.keys()}` \u2192 {\"a\": ScalarToken(\"a\")}. \u2713.\n\nEdge: what about the mapping in \"a: 1\\nb: two\" \u2014 the key \"a\" node is a scalar with str tag \u2192 construct_scalar \u2192 ScalarToken(\"a\", 0, 0)? start_mark.index=0, end_mark.index=1, end_index=0. string = content[0:1] = \"a\". \u2713.\n\nOK. Now, one thing I should double check: `token.value == {\"a\": 1, \"b\": \"two\"}` \u2014 DictToken._get_value. The get_info omitted the body. The real implementation (typesystem master):\n\n```python\n    def _get_value(self) -> typing.Any:\n        return {\n            key: token._get_value()\n            for key, token in self._child